## msc-gr | General relativity in depth | physics | 4 | phys3 | p3-astro
explain 5 | Geodesics: free fall is straight-line motion in curved spacetime
- In GR, a falling body feels no force: it follows a geodesic, the straightest possible path.
- $ d²xᵘ/dτ² + Γᵘ_αβ (dxᵅ/dτ)(dxᵝ/dτ) = 0
- *The Christoffel symbols Γ encode how the coordinates bend.*
> The Schwarzschild solution came from the trenches

story 5 | Schwarzschild solved Einstein's equations on the Russian front
- 1915: Karl Schwarzschild, serving in the German army, found the first exact solution for a spherical mass.
- He mailed it to Einstein, who was surprised an exact solution existed.
- *Schwarzschild died months later from an illness contracted at the front.*
> Gravitational time dilation at the horizon

quiz 5 | Schwarzschild radius of the Sun (2 × 10³⁰ kg)? r_s = 2GM/c²
* About 3 km
o About 3 m
o About 300 km
o About 30 cm
= 2 × 6.67 × 10⁻¹¹ × 2 × 10³⁰ / (9 × 10¹⁶) ≈ 2,960 m.
> Frame dragging

explain 5 | Spinning masses drag spacetime around
- The Lense–Thirring effect: a rotating mass twists nearby spacetime.
- Gravity Probe B measured Earth's frame dragging in 2011 using gyroscopes in orbit.
- *Near spinning black holes, the ergosphere forces everything to co-rotate.*
> The Friedmann equations

explain 5 | The Friedmann equations: the universe's expansion
- $ (ȧ/a)² = 8πGρ/3 − kc²/a² + Λc²/3
- a(t) is the scale factor of the universe; ρ its density; Λ the cosmological constant.
- *Einstein added Λ to get a static universe, later calling it his "biggest blunder". Today it's back, as dark energy.*
> Next: QFT in more depth

## msc-qft | Quantum field theory in depth | physics | 4 | phys3 | p3-qft
explain 5 | The Klein–Gordon equation
- $ (□ + m²c²/ħ²) φ = 0
- The relativistic wave equation for spin-0 particles, from E² = p²c² + m²c⁴.
- *It allows negative energies; only as a field theory does it make sense.*
> Gauge symmetry generates forces

explain 5 | Local gauge invariance creates the photon
- Demand the electron field's phase can change independently at every point.
- To keep the Lagrangian invariant, you must introduce a new field that compensates: the electromagnetic potential.
- *Forces aren't added by hand; they're required by symmetry.*
> Running couplings

explain 5 | Coupling constants change with energy
- The fine-structure constant α grows from about 1/137 at low energy to about 1/128 at the Z boson mass.
- The strong coupling shrinks at high energy: asymptotic freedom.
- *Gross, Wilczek and Politzer won the 2004 Nobel Prize for asymptotic freedom.*
> The Higgs mechanism

explain 5 | The Higgs mechanism, in one card
- The Higgs field's lowest-energy state isn't zero: it has a vacuum value everywhere.
- Particles interacting with it acquire mass, and the W and Z bosons absorb part of it.
- *The leftover ripple is the Higgs boson.*
> Next: condensed matter in more depth

## msc-cm | Condensed matter in depth | physics | 4 | phys3 | p3-solid
explain 5 | Phonons: quantised vibrations
- Lattice vibrations come in quanta called phonons.
- They carry heat in insulators and scatter electrons in metals (causing resistance).
- *Debye's model explains why solids' heat capacity falls like T³ at low temperature.*
> The quantum Hall effect

story 5 | The quantum Hall effect measures constants exactly
- 1980: von Klitzing found that in strong magnetic fields at low temperature, Hall resistance comes in perfectly flat steps of h/(ne²).
- The plateaus are so precise they're now used as resistance standards.
- *Nobel Prize 1985.* The steps are robust because of topology.
> Topological insulators

explain 5 | Topological insulators
- Insulating inside, conducting on the surface, guaranteed by the topology of the electron bands.
- Surface states can't be removed by smooth deformations, so they resist disorder.
- *Topological phases won the 2016 Nobel Prize (Thouless, Haldane, Kosterlitz).*
> Graphene

story 4 | Graphene was isolated with sticky tape
- 2004: Geim and Novoselov peeled graphite repeatedly with Scotch tape until single atomic layers remained.
- Graphene's electrons behave like massless particles described by a Dirac-like equation.
- *Nobel Prize 2010, just six years later.*
> Next: analysis and topology

## msc-ana | Measure theory and analysis | maths | 4 | calc1 | c1-ra2
explain 5 | Why Lebesgue integration
- Riemann integrates by slicing the x-axis. Lebesgue slices the y-axis: measure the set where f takes each value.
- The Dirichlet function (1 on rationals, 0 on irrationals) has no Riemann integral on [0, 1], but its Lebesgue integral is 0.
- *Lebesgue's theory makes limits and integrals swap under mild conditions.*
> Measure zero

explain 5 | The rationals have measure zero
- Cover each rational in [0, 1] with an interval of length ε/2ⁿ. Total length ≤ ε.
- So "almost every" real number is irrational.
- *Countable sets are negligible for integration.*
> The Cantor set

story 5 | The Cantor set: uncountable, but zero length
- Remove the middle third of [0, 1]. Then the middle thirds of what's left. Forever.
- What remains has total length zero, yet it's uncountable: as many points as the whole interval.
- *Its fractal dimension is log 2/log 3 ≈ 0.631.*
> Banach spaces

explain 5 | Function spaces are infinite-dimensional vector spaces
- L² functions (square-integrable) form a Hilbert space: an inner product and completeness.
- Quantum states live in Hilbert spaces.
- *Fourier series are just expansions in an orthonormal basis of L².*
> Next: topology

## msc-top | Topology | maths | 4 | calc3 | c3-mani
explain 4 | Open sets define topology
- A topology says which sets are "open". Continuity: preimages of open sets are open.
- No distances needed.
- *That's why topology can treat a doughnut and a mug as the same.*
> The fundamental group detects holes

explain 5 | The fundamental group
- Loops in a space, up to continuous deformation, form a group.
- The circle's fundamental group is the integers: how many times you wind around.
- *A sphere's is trivial: every loop shrinks to a point.*
> The Poincaré conjecture

story 5 | Perelman's proof of the Poincaré conjecture
- Conjecture (1904): every simply connected, closed 3-manifold is a 3-sphere.
- Perelman (2002–03) proved it using Hamilton's Ricci flow, which smooths a shape's geometry like heat.
- *He posted the proof online, declined the Fields Medal (2006) and the $1 million prize (2010).*
> Euler characteristic

quiz 4 | Euler characteristic V − E + F for any convex polyhedron?
* 2
o 0
o 1
o Depends on the shape
= Cube: 8 − 12 + 6. A torus-shaped polyhedron gives 0.
> Masters depth complete
